\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Spectral twins. A true fragment of a broken track (top) and an accidental false pair (bottom) have isomorphic compatibility graphs, hence identical $2\times 2$ sub-blocks of $A$, hence exactly the same eigenvalues and amplitudes. Every function of $A$ treats them identically; this is the floor theorem, and under noise it becomes the fragment floor of section\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:fit}.\relax }}{14}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:twins}{{8}{14}{Spectral twins. A true fragment of a broken track (top) and an accidental false pair (bottom) have isomorphic compatibility graphs, hence identical $2\times 2$ sub-blocks of $A$, hence exactly the same eigenvalues and amplitudes. Every function of $A$ treats them identically; this is the floor theorem, and under noise it becomes the fragment floor of section~\ref {sec:fit}.\relax }{figure.caption.8}{}}
